ikki:9098bf84aa8a6a1d12","definition":"To tolerate significant increases in throughput or other potentially limiting factors.","example":"That architecture won't scale to real-world environments.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"intransitive","wordFamily":["altered scale","at scale","broadscale","counterscale","diseconomies of scale","economies of scale","eigenscale","exascale","femtoscale","fieldscale","full-scale","gigascale","global-scale","grayscale","gray-scale","greyscale","Gunter's scale","gyroscale","hyperscale","industrial scale","Internet-scale","large-scale","logscale","long scale","macroscale","medium-scale","mesoscale","microscale","midscale","milliscale"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scale#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:c6d280c7ba31d3df9a","definition":"To handle heavily increasing loads without breaking down.","example":"We started using the term “scaling” in place of “growing” around the late 1990s to early 2000s, primarily due to the rise of venture-backed startups in Silicon Valley. The term migrated from tech/infrastructure (“can this system scale?”) to startups (“can this business scale?”)","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["altered scale","at scale","broadscale","counterscale","diseconomies of scale","economies of scale","eigenscale","exascale","femtoscale","fieldscale","full-scale","gigascale","global-scale","grayscale","gray-scale","greyscale","Gunter's scale","gyroscale","hyperscale","industrial scale","Internet-scale","large-scale","logscale","long scale","macroscale","medium-scale","mesoscale","microscale","midscale","milliscale"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scale#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:aa8c433dcd2ba02491","definition":"To increase revenues exponentially without proportional increases in costs.","example":"We started using the term “scaling” in place of “growing” around the late 1990s to early 2000s, primarily due to the rise of venture-backed startups in Silicon Valley. [...] VCs needed a way to describe businesses that could grow exponentially without adding proportional headcount or expense. So scalability became the holy grail. The term migrated from tech/infrastructure (“can this system scale?”) to startups (“can this business scale?”) and now everyone from lifestyle brands to DTC businesses use it as a universal term for \"make business go big.\" (By the mid-2010s, “scale” was in every pitch deck and founder tweet, even if what they really meant was: “We’re trying to 𝘨𝘳𝘰𝘸, but we want to sound smarter so we're using the term \"scale\".”) This change in language actually had a HUGE effect on the minds of the people who were operating businesses across the globe.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["altered scale","at scale","broadscale","counterscale","diseconomies of scale","economies of scale","eigenscale","exascale","femtoscale","fieldscale","full-scale","gigascale","global-scale","grayscale","gray-scale","greyscale","Gunter's scale","gyroscale","hyperscale","industrial scale","Internet-scale","large-scale","logscale","long scale","macroscale","medium-scale","mesoscale","microscale","midscale","milliscale"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scale#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:5b87ed7c8b282cef88","definition":"To weigh, measure or grade according to a scale or system.","example":"Scaling his present bearing with his past.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":["altered scale",